WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.410
Maddy: In this video we are going to
discuss how we can use customer feedback

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.240
to help improve an organisation.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.760
Phillip: Customer feedback isn’t especially
useful when it’s just taken on its own.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.379
For it to make sense and help your
organisation, you need to learn to be

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.299
able to interpret it and analyse it.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:22.680
When you start making real sense of customer
feedback, you’ll have a powerful tool for

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.540
continual organisation and product improvement.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.950
So, it’s important that you get this right.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.799
Maddy: It’s best to start by grouping the
feedback into different categories, such

00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:38.589
as customer service experience, website
experience, and product satisfaction.

00:00:38.589 --> 00:00:44.350
By grouping feedback like this, it is easier
to see the patterns that will start to emerge,

00:00:45.019 --> 00:00:49.600
such as does one category of feedback seem to
attract more negative comments than positive?

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.749
Phillip: You might also notice that customers
are happy with the product and their interactions

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.750
with customer service assistants, but are
not happy about the website experience.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:05.060
This can help you as you might have assumed
that customers really liked the website because

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:10.309
you personally approved it, but the feedback is
telling you a different story and the website

00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:12.970
is not as successful as the product or service.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.420
Maddy: It’s also important to always be objective
when looking at feedback, so try to stick

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.060
to the facts and figures, rather than just
speculating without any evidence, or just basing

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.450
things on your opinions or of those around you.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.170
The facts revealed by interpreting the
feedback are often a much more reliable

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.780
guide when it comes to improving how to do
things or making changes for the better.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.190
Phillip: Try looking for common themes or repeated
statements in the feedback you’re receiving.

00:01:41.099 --> 00:01:46.740
Remember if more than one person has had
the same issue, or negative experience

00:01:46.949 --> 00:01:50.870
then this is a problem to be addressed,
so you can find out what’s going on.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.890
Or maybe your attention is drawn to
positive experiences people have had, that

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:01.630
hadn’t been recognised before, such as
finding out about a new, important skill

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.990
that someone has, or an opportunity for
development and improvement is identified.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:12.320
Maddy: The volume of feedback can also be an
important piece of information in its own right.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.569
If you start receiving more feedback
across all the categories, either

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:21.070
negative or positive, this maybe an
indication that this needs your attention.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:25.100
Ask yourself, has there been a
big change in how the team works?

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.450
Phillip: Was there a new marketing promotion?

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.400
Maddy: Has your core product changed?

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:35.550
Phillip: Likewise, if the amount of
feedback starts to reduce in any or all

00:02:35.559 --> 00:02:40.840
areas, you should investigate what might
have caused that, and whether or not the

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.230
remaining feedback is positive or negative.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.180
Maddy: Sometimes the most important
feedback to address won’t be noticeable

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.359
by its amount but will be more significant
due to its strategic importance.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.060
Imagine if one or two users discover
that there’s a dramatically unsafe

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.479
feature in your main product.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.530
Even though only a small number of people
may have noticed this, it may mean that

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:07.800
everyone with that product might now
be at risk, without even realising it.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:13.769
This sort of feedback should be investigated
swiftly and often will involve a recall.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.859
Phillip: Finally, feedback doesn’t
do anything to help you on its own,

00:03:18.199 --> 00:03:19.660
even once you’ve interpreted it.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:23.940
It is the actual concrete steps that you
take, to act on the feedback that you

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.870
receive, that changes things and the best
way to do this, is to form action plans

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.040
with your team and other key stakeholders.

00:03:31.679 --> 00:03:35.640
Maddy: By structuring the action plan based
on objective facts derived from customer

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.270
feedback, you’re putting the customers’
insights to good use and improving your

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.200
organisation and future customer experiences.

